WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
This is a video about a mechanic I love, a&nbsp;
commander card I hate, and a bizarre deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.400
I built out of spite. Our tale begins in 1998,&nbsp;
with Urza’s Saga bringing all sorts of horrendous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.960
bullshit to the world, and also a gift—the cycling&nbsp;
mechanic. It’s a beautifully simple mechanic: you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.040
can pay an alternate cost on a card and discard&nbsp;
it in order to draw a card. This began in simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.240
fashion during Urza Block, but five years later&nbsp;
Onslaught block added a ton of dimension to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.840
mechanic. Varied cycle costs, triggers attached&nbsp;
to cycle abilities, cycling for basic land types,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.960
and six cards with triggered abilities when&nbsp;
other cards were cycled. These six were varying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:44.240
power levels, with one in particular being just…&nbsp;
excellent, but we’ll talk about that a bit later.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.480
Cycling has seen additional prints every&nbsp;
few years since, and for a simple reason:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.240
people like this mechanic. As an alternate cost&nbsp;
ability it adds a lot of flexibility to cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.600
which leads to interesting decisionmaking. Every&nbsp;
cycling card a player draws prompts the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:03.080
“is this card better than a different card plus&nbsp;
a small mana commitment?” This is nice in aggro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:07.040
when running out of gas might be a concern,&nbsp;
and it’s even more useful in a control deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.680
where that mana cost can be spent on the end of an&nbsp;
opponent’s turn, or even in response to things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:17.120
dig for answers. On top of this, since cycled&nbsp;
cards are discarded and go to the graveyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.200
they work well with graveyard synergies, providing&nbsp;
a near effortless way to put cards in the yard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.920
And that’s only the playstyles helped out&nbsp;
by the mechanic innately. Remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.960
cool card from Onslaught I mentioned? Astral&nbsp;
Slide has seen a ton of experimentation ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.080
since it was printed, as it synergizes with many&nbsp;
different cards and offers a ton of flexibility.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.840
As a result it was kind of understood as&nbsp;
the core of any cycling deck before 2020.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:44.960
Astral Slide decks have a super interesting&nbsp;
playstyle that revolves heavily around strategic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.760
resource management, and in a way that feels&nbsp;
very in-line with the fundamental nature of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:53.440
the mechanic. Number of cycling cards in hand&nbsp;
constantly dictates the strategic direction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.000
the deck—when you have more, you can get&nbsp;
bolder and set up for more daring turns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.400
as well as using the slide triggers more&nbsp;
liberally—as blocker elimination, to net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.960
small value off of ETBs, and so forth, or casting&nbsp;
a cycling card normally. By contrast, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.280
have fewer cycling cards in hand, each one is pure&nbsp;
gold. You always want to save them for protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:17.240
a critical creature or disrupting a dangerous&nbsp;
play from an opponent, but if you feel the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.920
slipping away you can also choose to gamble that&nbsp;
lifeline on an endstep for a little bit of value.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.080
In addition, mana is a super important resource&nbsp;
for both Astral Slide and the mechanic as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.400
as land counts also dictate how safe you feel&nbsp;
playing setup pieces, and how bursty your turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.720
can be. A more controlling cycling deck constantly&nbsp;
forces a player to think probabilistically—if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.960
spend mana to play a spell on my turn, what are&nbsp;
the odds I’ll be able to handle a key threat on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.200
my opponent’s turn if it gets played? What are&nbsp;
my odds of drawing an additional cycling card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.600
or an answer off the top? How much mana would I&nbsp;
need to have the best chance of averting death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.560
in a worst case scenario? These questions are&nbsp;
also fundamentally tied to deck construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.360
since a player must always weigh the strength of a&nbsp;
cycling card vs the cycling cost of it. As a card,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.760
Akroma’s Vengeance is wildly more powerful&nbsp;
than Winged Shepherd, but if I’m sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.200
there on an opponent’s turn with four mana&nbsp;
open, looking down the barrel of defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.120
I know which of the two I’d rather have in&nbsp;
hand. In short, the cycling mechanic creates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:21.520
promotes far more textured and strategic gameplay&nbsp;
than the vast majority of mechanics in MTG.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:29.240
With all of that deep strategic texture in mind,&nbsp;
let’s talk about Gavi, Nest Warden. It’s a 5 mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.600
2/5 who makes the first cycle each turn free,&nbsp;
and who creates a cat when you draw your second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:38.760
card each turn. This card is strong enough to be&nbsp;
a natural choice for a cycling commander, and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.800
deathly boring. Free cycles on each turn removes&nbsp;
an entire dimension of the cycling mechanic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.840
and on top of that it highly encourages a&nbsp;
stable quantity of cycling—one per turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:53.960
maybe two on an opponent’s turn sometimes. Every&nbsp;
cycle past that is a waste, since you have to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:58.400
for it and you don’t even get a cute little&nbsp;
dino-cat, and every turn you fail to cycle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.160
also a waste. C’mon, it’s free real estate. This&nbsp;
ability and the associated incentives remove the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.080
status of cycling cards as an important&nbsp;
resource to be saved and used carefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.840
and also in turn leave players with a lot of&nbsp;
extra mana they didn’t have to plan out. What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:19.960
does that mana get spent on? Well, permanents that&nbsp;
generate value when cards get cycled, of course.

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:23.760
Gavi destroys most of the cool, strategic&nbsp;
decision-making generally involved with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:28.160
playing a cycling deck, and replaces it with&nbsp;
yet another do-the-thing value-pile midrange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.960
deck. The design of most Gavi decks is such that&nbsp;
if you do the thing—cycling in this case—you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.880
an effortless mixture of card advantage&nbsp;
and board advantage spread out over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:43.240
just like the other 80% of EDH decks that exist&nbsp;
out there. And this isn’t because Gavi players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:48.880
are people who like especially boring decks—the&nbsp;
card is precision designed to encourage exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:54.040
this type of gameplay, even if you build from&nbsp;
scratch rather than buying the precon. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:58.720
a betrayal on the part of Wizards of the Coast&nbsp;
of everything cool about the cycling mechanic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.480
and it creates totally predictable&nbsp;
gameplay—well, except for one thing.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:10.600
You see, the other thing that Gavi incentivizes&nbsp;
is cards with high cycling costs. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:16.440
maybe the one thing about the card that’s not&nbsp;
dead-boring, but not really in a good way? Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.080
so this incentivization is most relevant for&nbsp;
one specific cycle of cards from Scourge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:24.240
and since Gavi doesn’t pay the&nbsp;
bonus cost of Decree of Justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:30.600
it’s actually mainly relevant for two cards.&nbsp;
The first is Decree of Silence. Big whoop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:36.120
it’s a free counterspell you’ll have sometimes.&nbsp;
Not much to say here. The other is Decree of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:43.000
Annihilation. I’ll here deliver a hot take—nuking&nbsp;
lands is maybe okay, maybe pretty cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:47.520
if your playgroup is down for that, and also if&nbsp;
you actually have a way of ending the game pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.040
soon after using that wild swing of momentum.&nbsp;
But nothing else about Gavi really promotes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.840
that sort of deck-style. More likely, you’ll&nbsp;
keep slowly getting cats, everybody’s manabase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:04.200
will gradually recover, and congratulations:&nbsp;
you’ve just doubled the length of this game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:10.720
Gavi into Decree of Annihilation, while certainly&nbsp;
unusual, still kinda fails to be interesting.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.520
So, we have here this pretty bland commander,&nbsp;
and if you ask an EDH player what they think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:21.000
about cycling, she’s what they think of. This cool&nbsp;
mechanic with a rich history and tons of synergy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:27.160
potential, reduced down to yet another value pile&nbsp;
commander. Now, to any Gavi players out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:31.920
I want to say: this is not an attack on you, and&nbsp;
Gavi is more interesting than a lot of commanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.160
out there. There are still some interesting&nbsp;
synergies and fun instant speed plays to be had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:42.360
piloting a Gavi deck. I don’t hate it because it’s&nbsp;
a uniquely bad design—it’s not—I hate it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.080
it’s a total betrayal of one of my favorite&nbsp;
mechanics. And this isn’t just about Gavi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:53.160
either. Wizards has a habit of printing what&nbsp;
I’ll call “designer commanders,” which I here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.520
use to mean commanders that take a particular&nbsp;
mechanic and fully dedicate themselves to it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:05.600
dictating a very specific and often very&nbsp;
boring type of deck based around that mechanic.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:11.400
Another example is Eligeth, Crossroads&nbsp;
Augur—taking scry, a neat top of library&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.360
manipulation mechanic, and literally just&nbsp;
replacing it with value in a way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:21.200
doesn’t synergize with anything related to the&nbsp;
original mechanic. Cool. These commanders push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.480
players toward less interesting decks, and their&nbsp;
specificity causes them to offer a narrow range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:31.640
possible decks to be built. A popular typographic&nbsp;
format for this sort of commander is one with two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:37.760
lines of text, one that says “whenever X happens,&nbsp;
Y happens” and another that says “whenever enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.800
Ys happen, a Z happens”. This is a template that&nbsp;
builds its own deck—you run cards that do X and Y,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:48.480
and maybe even some Z payoff if you’re feeling&nbsp;
nutty. Some people might enjoy this type of deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.600
and to those people I have to say: that’s great!&nbsp;
There are certainly people out there who want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.080
these kinds of cards—newer players, people who&nbsp;
don’t want to spend too long on deckbuilding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:01.000
or people who like to make unexpected versions&nbsp;
of seemingly straightforward commanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.560
and I think it’s cool that Wizards of&nbsp;
the Coast has given them lots of options&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:08.520
to work with. But if you want to try out an&nbsp;
alternative form of commander deck building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.520
I’m here to offer you a vision of what could be.&nbsp;
For the rest of this video, I’m going to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:19.240
about how I took one look at Gavi, and then turned&nbsp;
around and built a truly bizarre cycling deck.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:24.000
It started with me absentmindedly cruising through&nbsp;
random Onslaught cards looking for deck ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.920
as one does, and as expected I saw Astral Slide,&nbsp;
but also another enchantment I wasn’t familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:35.440
with—Overbooked Cemetery. These seem kinda&nbsp;
neat together, right? One promotes cycling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.920
the other works well after I cycle creatures&nbsp;
into the graveyard? With these in my pocket I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.840
decided to include blue as well, since it’s a&nbsp;
very useful color for both cycling and instant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:50.640
speed shenanigans, and I started shopping around&nbsp;
for a commander in Esper colors. My hope was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.960
find a commander that could be a flexible value&nbsp;
engine when I flickered cards with Astral Slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:00.360
and this was my shortlist. Eventually, I&nbsp;
decided all of them were either too generic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.960
too niche, or not the right vibe,&nbsp;
all except good ol’ Sharuum.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:11.160
With my commander selected, I set some healthy&nbsp;
ground rules: no Sculpting Steel type combos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:17.280
and a $100 budget. I’m not building a high-power&nbsp;
Sharuum deck, I’m building an Astral Slide deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.960
A good place to start is always wincons, and one&nbsp;
that leapt out immediately was Magister Sphinx,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.520
which combined with Sharuum can one-shot a&nbsp;
player. Sharuum’s nature as a reanimation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.720
tool means that a beatdown suite&nbsp;
with a number of big flying artifact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:35.160
creatures is likely to be a good bet at&nbsp;
hundred-dollar-cycling-deck power levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:41.240
and will hopefully offer enough beef to win even&nbsp;
when Magister Sphinx isn’t available. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.360
this beatdown step will likely be tricky unless&nbsp;
I’m mostly stabilized against hate and attacks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.280
which can be accomplished through&nbsp;
my core card of Astral Slide.

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:55.080
The card offers so many use cases—it’s&nbsp;
a reanimation engine with Sharuum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.600
it can be a value, removal, ramp, or lifegain&nbsp;
engine with other artifact creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.120
and that’s to say nothing of the built-in&nbsp;
protection and disruption utility of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:07.960
card. Since Astral Slide is so key to the&nbsp;
deck and I can only run two in the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:12.480
enchantment tutors were a must, so I started&nbsp;
out with Idyllic Tutor, and eventually settled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:19.960
on Zur as my other tutor. His wizard tag is oddly&nbsp;
important, as WotC has printed two Wizardcycling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:25.360
cards—basically instant speed wizard tutors&nbsp;
that count as a cycle. Out of these strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:30.880
cards organically grew a toolbox that expanded as&nbsp;
I found new hurdles my deck needed to overcome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:36.800
Wizardcycling grabs a tutor for slide, a tutor for&nbsp;
my top end boys, a recursion spell for my slides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.160
and a stifle effect that’s also an extra&nbsp;
cycle for my big turns. A mixture of tutoring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:47.160
discard outlets, and my recursion spell commander&nbsp;
means that my deck’s key pieces generally get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:52.040
play mana-efficiently. The deck does a great&nbsp;
job scratching my itch to play a complex and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:57.720
strategic deck, and it does so at a middling power&nbsp;
level that feels good in quite a lot of pools.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:01.600
A key takeaway from this deckbuilding&nbsp;
process for me was the strength of flexible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:06.560
ground up deck construction. By starting with&nbsp;
a mechanic and a couple key cards and selecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.200
a commander afterward, my deck ended up with a&nbsp;
foundation that includes the commander but doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:16.320
wholly consist of the commander. It’s built to&nbsp;
follow general best practices for cycling decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:20.760
and though it does include the occasional stormy&nbsp;
element or value engine, each of these pieces are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:25.720
intentional, and most of them are tutorable if&nbsp;
I decide that’s what a given situation demands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.640
The most important thing I prefer to avoid is&nbsp;
having the entire deck stand on the shoulders of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:34.280
the commander, at least not unless you’re ready&nbsp;
for that sort of responsibility. I could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:38.920
made the commander Zur, but then the issue of not&nbsp;
having a consistent value source combined with Zur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:44.560
being a removal magnet would have rapidly turned&nbsp;
my deck into one more Zur than Astral Slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.520
as draw enchantments and protection spells gobble&nbsp;
up my deck space. It’s good to keep in mind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.800
sort of deckbuilding incentives a given commander&nbsp;
pushes onto you, as even one that seems like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:58.280
good idea on paper might make your deck make&nbsp;
a turn that isn’t what you were looking for.

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:03.160
If I was to wrap this up in a bow, I’d go&nbsp;
with this: when you’re building a deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.520
figure out what the deck wants to do, and try to&nbsp;
turn that into a somewhat focused plan. Accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:11.720
that you won’t nail the first version of the&nbsp;
deck, and that there’s room to adapt as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:17.040
encounter further problems. I’d say there&nbsp;
are four key versions of any single deck:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:21.000
the first principles version in your brain,&nbsp;
the draft version after you actually hammer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:26.080
out a 100 card list, the streamlined version&nbsp;
completed after you solo playtest a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:30.880
and the “final” version completed after you&nbsp;
play it against other people for a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:36.960
Each of these steps will solve a different set of&nbsp;
problems that the deck has, and that’s a strength.

